// The review's exact forged-handle program (task brief for
// 2026-08-08-joinhandle-task-identity, headline case). `JoinHandle`'s field
// is public, so nothing stops constructing one directly instead of going
// through `spawn` -- naming a future that was never handed to the executor.
//
// Before this fix `JoinHandle` also carried an `Int` id, and
// `task_is_done`/`task_release` looked a task up by that id rather than by
// the future itself. Task ids are handed out `0, 1, 2, ...` in spawn order,
// `block_on`'s own root task is always spawned first, and a default-valued
// `Int` field reads as `0` -- so this exact program named the root task
// itself and `join`'s `while !task_is_done(id) { yield_now().await }` loop
// waited forever for the task that was doing the waiting. `nova check`
// accepted it and `nova run` hung with empty stdout and stderr (see ADR
// 0009, which discusses this same program).
//
// Keyed on the future's own state address instead of an id, there is no
// task at all for an unspawned future to misresolve to, so this now aborts
// with a diagnostic instead of hanging.
async fn spin() -> Int { 1 }
async fn run() -> Int {
    let h = JoinHandle { fut: spin() }
    h.join().await
}
fn main() { println("total ${block_on(run())}") }
